## Solution

We interpret the problem as follows: among all linear (affine) polynomials $p(x) = a + cx$, find the value of the slope $c$ that minimizes the uniform (max) error
$$\Phi(a,c) \;=\; \max_{x\,\in\,[-\pi/2,\,\pi/2]}\bigl|\cos x - (a+cx)\bigr|.$$

### Key observation: symmetry

The function $\cos x$ is **even**, and the interval $[-\pi/2,\pi/2]$ is **symmetric** about $0$.

For any candidate $p(x)=a+cx$, consider its reflection $\bar p(x)=a-cx$. Then
$$\bigl|\cos x - \bar p(x)\bigr|=\bigl|\cos x-(a-cx)\bigr|=\bigl|\cos(-x)-(a+c(-x))\bigr|,$$
which is just the error of $p$ evaluated at $-x$. Hence, by the change of variable $x\mapsto -x$,
$$\Phi(a,-c)=\Phi(a,c). \qquad\text{($\Phi$ is even in $c$)}$$

### Convexity argument

$\Phi(a,c)$ is convex in $(a,c)$, since it is a supremum of convex functions (each $|\cos x-(a+cx)|$ is convex in $(a,c)$, and the supremum of convex functions is convex).

Fix $a$. By evenness, $\Phi(a,c)=\Phi(a,-c)$. By convexity,
$$\Phi(a,0)=\Phi\!\left(a,\tfrac{c+(-c)}{2}\right)\le \tfrac12\Phi(a,c)+\tfrac12\Phi(a,-c)=\Phi(a,c).$$
So for every $a$ and every $c$,
$$\Phi(a,0)\le \Phi(a,c).$$

Therefore the global minimizer of $\Phi$ may be taken with **$c=0$**; any nonzero slope can be replaced by $0$ without increasing the error. In other words, the best degree‑$1$ uniform approximation to an even function on a symmetric interval is itself even — a constant.

### Identifying the minimizer (for completeness)

With $c=0$ we minimize $\max_{x\in[-\pi/2,\pi/2]}|\cos x-a|$. On this interval $\cos x$ decreases from $1$ (at $x=0$) to $0$ (at $x=\pm\pi/2$), so its range is $[0,1]$. The best constant approximation to a function with range $[0,1]$ is the midpoint $a=\tfrac12$, giving max error $\tfrac12$. This is the unique minimizer, and it has slope $0$.

### Conclusion

The value of $c$ that minimizes the maximum norm of $\cos x-(a+cx)$ over $[-\pi/2,\pi/2]$ is

$$\boxed{\,c=0\,}.$$

